Summary:
If you've ever wanted to know what it would be like to live through one of the worst days of your life—but in a funny way, not like that—then this movie could be for you. Also, you may be interested in seeing what the cast of the original "House Party" is up to these days.
These days, movies seem to come out two ways: either they’re so unforgettable that people remember them forever (like “The Dark Knight”, “Toy Story”, or “District 9”), or they are so terrible that audiences never, ever forget them (such as, well, pretty much everything else). “House Party 3” definitely falls into the latter category. After watching it, I felt like I had somehow managed to survive one of the worst days of my life, but in a way that was amusing rather than upsetting or depressing. This is because unlike “Carnage Park”, “The Room”, or “Fifty Shades of Grey”, the movie isn’t actually bad—it just has some serious flaws that make it extremely difficult to watch. These include:
• A storyline that makes absolutely no sense;
• An overabundance of unnecessary characters and events;
• Numerous instances of slapstick comedy;
• Lots of swearing;
• Stupid jokes and/or pop culture references throughout;
• An over-the-top performance from Christopher Reid (who plays “Shawn”);
All these things combined turn what could have been a funny, entertaining party flick into something truly awful. That being said, there is still some good stuff here, such as:
• Great cameos from MC Hammer (as himself) and Vanilla Ice (playing himself);
• A surprisingly effective rap song written by Hammer specifically for this film titled “Getting’ Paid”;
• A scene where Hammer performs at the titular house party despite having been previously hired to play somewhere else…
Okay fine, maybe not worth watching unless you really want to see just how terrible this movie could possibly get. So, yeah: watch at your own risk!
This movie is pretty much only interesting because of the actors involved. Other than that, it doesn’t really stand out. It’s just not very memorable. It gets worse
